[2026-07-23 02:37:06] local.INFO: AI StoreResultStep completed - Diagnosis workflow {"result":{"issue_type":"pipe_leak","urgency":"medium","estimated_price_min":1500,"estimated_price_max":5000,"confidence":0.7,"summary":"Water leaking under your sink is a medium-urgency issue that needs attention to prevent damage. It's usually caused by loose connections or worn parts. You can try to locate and tighten the leak after turning off the water. If the leak persists or you can't find it, call a professional. Expect basic repairs to cost between NPR 1,500 and NPR 5,000.","recommended_service":"Leak Repair","customer_explanation":"You have water dripping or collecting under your sink. This is a common issue and usually means one of the pipes or connections is not holding water properly. It's important to address this quickly to prevent water damage and mold in your cabinet.","likely_cause":"Most often, this happens because of a loose connection where pipes join, a worn-out rubber seal (gasket) inside a connection, a small crack in a drain pipe, or even a leak from the faucet itself or its water supply hoses.","risk_level":"medium","immediate_actions":["**Catch the water:** Place a bucket, large pot, or towels under the leak immediately to catch the water and prevent further damage to your cabinet or floor.","**Turn off water supply:** Find the small valves under the sink (usually two, one for hot and one for cold) and turn them clockwise to shut off the water flow to the sink. If you can't find these, locate your house's main water shut-off valve and turn off the water to the entire house.","**Wipe down the area:** Carefully wipe down the wet area with a dry cloth. This will help you spot exactly where the leak is starting from."],"diy_checks":["**Inspect the drain pipes:** Look closely at the curved pipe (P-trap) and all the connections leading from your sink drain. Feel around with a dry paper towel for moisture, especially at the joints. Check if the nuts connecting the pipes are loose.","**Check the water supply lines:** These are the flexible hoses coming out of the wall that connect to your faucet. Check if they are wet or dripping, especially where they connect to the faucet or the small shut-off valves.","**Examine the faucet base:** Sometimes, water can leak from the base of the faucet on top of the sink and run down into the cabinet below. Look for any water accumulation right at the base of the faucet.","**Gently tighten connections:** If you find a slightly loose nut on the drain pipes or water supply lines, try to gently tighten it with an adjustable wrench. Do not overtighten, as this can cause more damage."],"required_tools":["Bucket or large pot","Flashlight","Old rags or towels","Adjustable wrench"],"professional_steps":["**Accurate leak detection:** A professional plumber has specialized tools and experience to quickly and accurately find the exact source of the leak, even if it's hidden.","**Component replacement:** They can safely replace faulty washers, seals, gaskets, or sections of damaged pipes that are causing the leak.","**Faucet or supply line repair/replacement:** If the leak is from the faucet or its supply lines, they can repair or install new, reliable components.","**Secure connections:** Plumbers ensure all connections are properly tightened and sealed, using the correct materials like Teflon tape or pipe dope, to prevent future leaks."],"safety_precautions":["**Always shut off water:** Make sure to turn off the water supply (either under the sink or to the whole house) before attempting any inspection or repair.","**Do not overtighten:** When tightening nuts, do so gently. Overtightening can strip threads, crack plastic pipes, or damage fittings, making the problem worse.","**Wear gloves:** Wear protective gloves to keep your hands clean and safe, especially if the water is dirty or if you're dealing with older pipes.","**Call a professional if unsure:** If you can't find the source of the leak, or if you're not comfortable performing the DIY checks, do not attempt to disassemble pressurized pipes or make repairs you're unsure about. It's always safer to call a licensed plumber."],"preventive_maintenance":["**Regular visual checks:** Periodically open your under-sink cabinet and quickly check for any signs of moisture, drips, or stains on the cabinet base. Catching small leaks early can prevent bigger problems.","**Avoid chemical drain cleaners:** Harsh chemical drain cleaners can corrode pipes and fittings over time, leading to leaks. Use natural methods like boiling water and baking soda for minor clogs, or a plumber's snake.","**Don't hang heavy items:** Avoid hanging heavy bags or items on the drain pipes under the sink, as this can strain connections and cause them to loosen or crack."]}} 
